  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Neighbourhood policing teams, Serious Organised Crime Agency, tougher penalties on guns and knives, robust counter-terror laws while upholding liberties; secure borders with biometrics and ID cards and a points-based migration system; NATO at the core of defence and a retained independent nuclear deterrent; strong discipline and fair admissions in schools; rights matched by responsibilities, incapacity benefit reform and make-work-pay policies; strict fiscal rules and a 2% inflation target; major NHS investment, waiting-time guarantees, cleanliness drives and smoke-free workplaces; Single Equality Act and Commission on Equality and Human Rights. TEXT_D: Repeal control orders, scrap ID cards, curb Prime Ministerial power, devolution and STV; 10,000 more police and a UK Border Force but with fewer central targets and stronger civil-liberty safeguards; replace Council Tax with a Local Income Tax and add a 50% rate over £100k; abolish tuition fees and provide free personal care, eye and dental checks; allow asylum seekers to work; strong green agenda including a Carbon Tax, GM safeguards, and no new nuclear; retain a minimum nuclear deterrent but oppose the Iraq war and seek withdrawal via the UN.",
  "reasoning": "With Authority weighted highest and moderate emphasis on Loyalty and Proportionality, TEXT_E aligns better by prioritizing law-and-order, border security (biometrics/ID cards), counter-terror powers, NATO and the nuclear deterrent, school discipline, and a \"rights matched by responsibilities\" framing, while still delivering substantial Care and moderate Equality. TEXT_D emphasizes Care and Equality but foregrounds civil-liberties limits on state authority, devolution, and environmental purity, mapping less well to a profile that prizes Authority and Proportionality and places relatively low weight on Sanctity.",
